title: Collator protocol — honor the scheduling lookahead, not a claim-queue/ancestry length

doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    Both validator sides of the collator protocol derived the claim-queue window length from a
    value that is only a *lower bound* on the runtime scheduling lookahead, and so truncated the
    tail of the claim queue — silently dropping advertisement positions that are in fact
    fetchable.

    * Experimental (`validator_side_experimental`) used the allowed-ancestry path length.
    * Legacy (`validator_side`, `is_slot_available`) used the claim-queue length.

    Either bound is below the true scheduling lookahead in two production-reachable situations:

    * near genesis (the chain is shorter than the lookahead), and
    * for the first `lookahead - 1` blocks after a **session change**, where the implicit view
      clamps ancestry at the session boundary (the relay chain cannot back candidates from a
      previous session), so the allowed-ancestry path is shorter than the lookahead.

    In those windows, advertisements landing on the tail claim-queue positions — reachable from
    the in-session scheduling parents, including the leaf itself — were rejected, reducing
    collation throughput for a few blocks after every session rotation.

    Fix: fetch the scheduling lookahead from the runtime (`SchedulingLookahead`) per active leaf
    and use it as the window bound in both validator sides. The per-leaf claim queues and the
    lookahead are now carried together in a shared `LeafClaimQueues` type, fetched in one place
    so the lookahead can never again be inferred from a length.

    No public API changes; behavior change is confined to the collator-protocol validator sides.

crates:
- name: polkadot-collator-protocol
  bump: patch
